"1. Lancet Infect Dis. 2022 Nov;22(11):1528-1530. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(22)00683-1.\n\nThe WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Book: providing guidance on optimal use and informing \npolicy.\n\nSharland M(1), Cappello B(2), Ombajo LA(3), Bazira J(4), Chitatanga R(5), Chuki \nP(6), Gandra S(7), Harbarth S(8), Loeb M(9), Mendelson M(10), Moja L(2), Pulcini \nC(11), Tacconelli E(12), Zanichelli V(2), Zeng M(13), Huttner BD(2).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Centre for Neonatal and Paediatric Infection, Institute for Infection and \nImmunity, St George's University of London, London, UK. Electronic address: \nmsharland@sgul.ac.uk.\n(2)Access to Medicines and Health Products Division, World Health Organisation, \nGeneva, Switzerland.\n(3)Department of Clinical Medicine and Therapeutics, University of Nairobi, \nNairobi, Kenya.\n(4)Department of Microbiology and Parasitology, Mbarara University of Science \n&amp; Technology, Mbarara, Uganda.\n(5)Antimicrobial Resistance National Coordinating Centre, Public Health \nInstitute of Malawi, Malawi.\n(6)Antimicrobial Stewardship Unit, Jigme Dorji Wangchuck National Referral \nHospital, Thimphu, Bhutan.\n(7)Department of Internal Medicine, Division of Infectious Diseases, Washington \nUniversity School of Medicine, Saint Louis, MO, USA.\n(8)Infection Control Programme and WHO Collaborating Centre on Patient Safety, \nUniversity of Geneva Hospitals and Faculty of Medicine, Geneva, Switzerland.\n(9)Department of Medicine, and Michael G DeGroote Institute for Infectious \nDisease Research, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario, Canada.\n(10)Division of Infectious Diseases and HIV Medicine, Department of Medicine, \nUniversity of Cape Town, Cape Town, South Africa.\n(11)APEMAC, and Infectious Diseases Department, Universit\u00e9 de Lorraine, Nancy, \nFrance.\n(12)Infectious Diseases Unit, Department of Diagnostics and Public Health, \nUniversity of Verona, Verona, Italy.\n(13)Department of Infectious Diseases, Children's Hospital of Fudan University, \nShanghai, China.\n\nComment in\n    Lancet Infect Dis. 2023 Feb;23(2):152-153. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(23)00002-6.\n\nDOI: 10.1016/S1473-3099(22)00683-1\nPMID: 36309019 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: MS reports grants from: (1) the ADILA project \n(Antimicrobial Resistance, Prescribing, and Consumption Data to Inform Country \nAntibiotic Guidance and Local Action; funder, Wellcome Trust; grant holder, St \nGeorges; April, 2022\u2013October, 2025); (2) Impact of oral step-down to amoxicillin \nor co-amoxiclav and of duration of antibiotic therapy on effectiveness, safety \nand selection of antibiotic resistance in severe childhood community-acquired \npneumonia (CAP): a randomised controlled trial (PediCAP; funder, EDCTP; grant \nholder, PENTA; April 2014\u2013March, 2024; and (3) NeoAMR Global Neonatal Sepsis \nObservational Study (NeoOBS): a prospective cohort study of sepsis in \nhospitalised neonates (funder, DNDi; grant holder, GARDP; September, \n2017\u2013August, 2024, (\u20ac1\u2008826\u2008618 total) \u2013 Chief Investigator (5%). MS also reports \nroles as Member of the WHO Essential Medicines List (EML) Committee (from 2016) \nand Chair of the EML Antibiotic Working Group (from 2019). ML reports \nparticipation on advisory boards for Sunovion pharmaceuticals (payment for \nadvisory board on pneumonia) and Paladin Labs (payment for advisory board on \ndalbavancin). He also reports payments to him, and McMaster University made by \nthe WHO EML team. SH reports grants paid by the European Commission to his \ninstitution (University of Geneva Hospitals and Faculty of Medicine, Geneva, \nSwitzerland). CP reports that she currently works for the French Ministry of \nHealth, coordinating the Antimicrobial Resistance National Action Plan. All \nother authors declare no competing interests."